---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import ScrollTable from '../../components/ScrollTable.astro';
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';

const options = [
  { name: 'seed', type: '#rgb or #rrggbb', text: 'The source color. Required.' },
  {
    name: 'variant',
    type: "'tonalSpot' (default), 'expressive', 'vibrant', 'neutral', 'monochrome', 'fidelity', 'content', 'rainbow', 'fruitSalad'",
    text: "Material Color Utilities' dynamic scheme: how far roles move from the seed's hue and chroma.",
  },
  {
    name: 'mode',
    type: "'system' (default), 'light', 'dark'",
    text: 'system writes both schemes and follows prefers-color-scheme; data-theme overrides it.',
  },
  { name: 'contrast', type: "-1 to 1, or 'standard' (0), 'medium', 'high'", text: 'Contrast level. Spec 2025 treats negative values as 0.' },
  { name: 'specVersion', type: "'2025' (default), '2021'", text: 'The color spec (DECISIONS D3); 2025 adds the dim roles.' },
  { name: 'motionScheme', type: "'expressive' (default), 'standard'", text: 'Points the spring tokens at one motion scheme.' },
  { name: 'coreColors', type: 'secondary, tertiary, neutral, neutralVariant, error', text: 'Replace a derived palette with one built from your color.' },
  { name: 'overrides', type: '{ light?: {...}, dark?: {...} }', text: "Per-mode role overrides, e.g. { light: { primary: '#6750a4' } }." },
  { name: 'selector', type: 'string', text: 'A single compound selector to scope the theme to. Default :root.' },
];
---

<Guide
  title="Theming"
  description="Every component reads color, type, shape and motion from CSS custom properties. Generate a full light and dark color scheme from one seed color, at build time or at runtime."
  summary="Theme every component with CSS custom properties, and generate light and dark color schemes from one seed color, at build time or at runtime."
>
  <h2 id="baseline">The baseline theme</h2>
  <p>
    <code>css/index.css</code> (or <code>@kyralabs/m3e-tokens/theme.css</code>) sets Material's
    baseline purple scheme, the colors of Compose's <code>expressiveLightColorScheme()</code> and
    <code>darkColorScheme()</code>. It is light, dark when the system prefers dark, and forced
    with <code>data-theme</code> on the root element:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="html"
    label="data-theme example"
    code={`<html lang="en" data-theme="dark">  <!-- or "light"; leave it off to follow the system -->`}
  />
  <p>
    Add <code>{'<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">'}</code> so form controls and
    scrollbars follow the scheme too. To let readers switch, set the attribute from a script and
    remember the choice; run that script in the <code>{'<head>'}</code> so the page never paints in
    the wrong scheme first:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    label="theme switch script"
    code={`// In an inline <script> in <head>:
const saved = localStorage.getItem('theme'); // 'light' | 'dark' | null (system)
if (saved) document.documentElement.dataset.theme = saved;

// From your theme switch:
function setTheme(mode) {
  if (mode === 'system') delete document.documentElement.dataset.theme;
  else document.documentElement.dataset.theme = mode;
  localStorage.setItem('theme', mode);
}`}
  />

  <h2 id="seed">A theme from a seed color</h2>
  <p>
    <code>createTheme</code> builds every color role from one seed with Material Color Utilities
    0.4.0 and returns the CSS. Its rules are unlayered, so loading them after the library's CSS
    overrides the baseline theme:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    label="createTheme example"
    code={`import { createTheme } from '@kyralabs/m3exp-web/theme';

const { cssText, tokens } = createTheme({ seed: '#6750A4', variant: 'expressive' });

const style = document.createElement('style');
style.textContent = cssText;
document.head.append(style);

tokens.light?.primary; // the resolved roles, e.g. for a canvas or an email template`}
  />
  <p>
    The home page's <a href="/">theme demo</a> runs exactly this: pick a hue and a variant and the
    whole site re-themes.
  </p>
  <ScrollTable label="createTheme options">
    <table>
      <caption class="site-sr-only">createTheme options</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Values</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          options.map((o) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>{o.name}</code></th>
              <td>{o.type}</td>
              <td>{o.text}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ScrollTable>

  <h3 id="cli">At build time</h3>
  <p>
    The <code>m3e-theme</code> command from <code>@kyralabs/m3e-tokens</code> writes the same CSS,
    so no color code ships to the browser:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="sh"
    label="m3e-theme command"
    code={`npx m3e-theme --seed "#6750A4" --variant expressive > theme.css
npx m3e-theme --seed "#6750A4" --mode dark --contrast medium --out theme-dark.css`}
  />
  <p>
    Flags: <code>--variant</code>, <code>--mode</code>, <code>--contrast</code>,
    <code>--spec</code>, <code>--motion</code>, <code>--selector</code>, <code>--out</code>, and the
    core colors <code>--secondary</code>, <code>--tertiary</code>, <code>--neutral</code>,
    <code>--neutral-variant</code>, <code>--error</code>. Load the file after the library's CSS.
  </p>

  <h3 id="core-colors">Core colors</h3>
  <p>
    Like Material Theme Builder, you can give your own colors for the other palettes. Each keeps
    the given color's hue and chroma; the variant still maps roles to tones. A red primary on grey
    surfaces:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    label="core colors example"
    code={`createTheme({
  seed: '#d84848',
  variant: 'fidelity',
  coreColors: { neutral: '#808080', neutralVariant: '#808080' },
});`}
  />
  <p>
    With spec 2025 (the <code>tonalSpot</code>, <code>neutral</code>, <code>vibrant</code> and
    <code>expressive</code> variants) the outline and surface-variant roles come from the neutral
    palette, so <code>neutralVariant</code> has no effect there. <code>variant: 'fidelity'</code>
    keeps the seed closest to how you gave it, like Theme Builder.
  </p>

  <h3 id="scoped">Scoped themes</h3>
  <p>
    <code>selector</code> applies a theme to part of a page. Its dark rules key off
    <code>data-theme</code> on the same element:
  </p>
  <p class="site-note">
    Known issue (<a href="https://github.com/kyra-labs/m3exp-web/issues/120">#120</a>): inside a
    scoped theme, components keep the page theme's colors, because their color tokens resolve at
    <code>:root</code>. Elements that read the <code>--m3e-sys-color-*</code> roles directly
    follow the scope.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    label="scoped theme example"
    code={`const { cssText } = createTheme({ seed: '#00696d', selector: '.promo' });
// .promo { … } and .promo[data-theme="dark"] { … }, plus the system dark rule`}
  />

  <h2 id="tokens">Beyond color</h2>
  <p>
    Type, shape, motion, elevation and spacing are tokens too:
    <code>--m3e-sys-typescale-*</code>, <code>--m3e-sys-shape-*</code>,
    <code>--m3e-sys-motion-*</code>, <code>--m3e-sys-elevation-*</code>,
    <code>--m3e-sys-space-*</code>, and per component <code>--m3e-comp-&lt;component&gt;-*</code>.
    Override any of them in your own CSS; read them in yours instead of hard-coding values:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="css"
    label="token usage example"
    code={`.headline {
  color: var(--m3e-sys-color-on-surface);
  font-family: var(--m3e-sys-typescale-headline-medium-font);
  font-size: var(--m3e-sys-typescale-headline-medium-size);
  line-height: var(--m3e-sys-typescale-headline-medium-line-height);
}`}
  />
</Guide>
